  "captions": {
    "fig6a_final_stage_profile": "Figure 6a: Profiling our own engine shows that single-threaded argpartition takes 66% of the exact batched search (10.23 of 15.55 ms) - the serial fraction of Amdahl's law; a parallel Numba top-k cuts it to 5.36 ms and the final SVD-128 + re-rank(100) + parallel top-k engine needs 3.67 ms. Without OMP_WAIT_POLICY=PASSIVE, Numba's spinning OpenMP threads slow the following BLAS matmul from 4.4 to 34.1 ms (two thread pools competing for the same cores).",
    "fig6_scaling": "Figure 6: Search time grows at most linearly with corpus size (log-log slope 0.54 for the final engine between 5K and 20K and 0.85 up to 200K synthetic documents - below 1 because fixed per-call costs still matter), while the fused Numba kernel, which re-reads the corpus for every query, slows down x36 for 10x data once the corpus leaves the 96 MB L3 cache; the final engine answers 200 queries over 20K documents in 3.89 ms and a single query in 0.21 ms.",
    "fig7_all_versions": "Figure 7: From 154 s (naive Python) to 3.89 ms (Final: SVD-128 + re-rank(100) + parallel top-k) on the same 200x20K workload, a 39,689x speed-up; Python-level fixes alone reach at most 4x - the big jump comes from leaving the interpreter (BLAS / Numba)."
